<Chapter title="Start light" slug="start-light">
  <section class="chapter">
    <div class="head-eyebrow">10 · Adoption</div>
    <h2>Start light</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      You don't adopt all of Specline at once, but there are no tiers to pick between —
      it's one system throughout. Adopt Part 1, then Part 2; Part 3 isn't a further
      stage, it's a switch.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="book-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it adds</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Part 1 — writing a spec</strong></td><td>One spec, in the Specline shape, in <code>docs/drafts/</code> or <code>docs/specs/</code>, with integrity checks enabled. An afternoon, to feel whether the structure helps before committing.</td><td>Trying it</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Part 2 — keeping the record</strong></td><td>The four-folder lifecycle, the decider's go in git, building, review, graduation, <code>archive/</code> and <code>knowledge/</code>. The whole methodology for a single stream of work.</td><td><strong>Start here</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Part 3 — unattended builds</strong> <em>(experimental)</em></td><td>Parallel-work governance — staleness timers, the decider budget, the coupling ceiling. For running many builds nobody is watching, at once.</td><td>Later, if ever</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      Parts 1 and 2 are where almost everyone lives. It's the full shape-approve-build-review-graduate
      loop, and it's enough to hand an agent a feature and trust what comes back. Part 3 only
      starts earning its keep when you have enough features and agents in flight that you need
      to govern <em>which</em> work happens and how the queue is bounded — concerns that are
      pure noise for a solo planner with one feature at a time. It's off by default; flip
      <code>unattended: true</code> in <code>specline.yml</code> when you actually run builds
      nobody is watching.
    </p>
    <div class="callout"><span class="ci">▸</span><p>If a Part-3 rule isn't earning its keep, leave the switch off. That's a setting to change, not a failure to push through.</p></div>
    <h3>How you actually start</h3>
    <p>
      The setup is almost nothing. Drop the brief into your repo, open a fresh chat, and say
      <em>"let's shape a feature."</em> The agent pulls the methodology itself, runs the moves
      from chapter 6, and checks its own work with Specline as it goes. You bring the product
      judgment; it brings the shape. From there, the rest of the handbook is just what happens
      next.
    </p>
    <div class="callout"><span class="ci">▸</span><p>Reference: <a href="/docs/setup">getting set up</a> and the <a href="/docs/reference/config">repo configuration</a> — where you tune what Specline enforces and flip the unattended switch.</p></div>
  </section>
</Chapter>
